  "content": {
    "id": "p7-q8-root",
    "type": "question",
    "label": null,
    "prompt": "Evaluate\n$$\\frac{1}{10^{-10}+1}+\\frac{1}{10^{-9}+1}+\\cdots+\\frac{1}{10^9+1}+\\frac{1}{10^{10}+1}.$$",
    "layout": "list",
    "columns": null,
    "questionDiagrams": [],
    "children": [],
    "answer": {
      "short": "$\\dfrac{21}{2}$.\n\nPair terms with opposite exponents; each pair sums to $1$, and the zero-exponent term is $\\dfrac12$.",
      "worked": "For $k=1,2,\\ldots,10$,\n$$\\begin{align*}\\frac{1}{10^{-k}+1}+\\frac{1}{10^k+1}&=\\frac{10^k}{1+10^k}+\\frac{1}{10^k+1}\\\\\\frac{1}{10^{-k}+1}+\\frac{1}{10^k+1}&=1.\\end{align*}$$\nThere are ten such pairs. The remaining term is\n$$\\frac{1}{10^0+1}=\\frac12.$$\nHence the sum is\n$$10+\\frac12=\\frac{21}{2}.$$",
      "solutionDiagrams": []
    },
    "answerSpaceMm": 64
  },
